","executionInfo":{"status":"ok","timestamp":1718023368518,"user_tz":-330,"elapsed":10,"user":{"displayName":"Akhil Teja BM","userId":"02238052875404682489"}}},"execution_count":27,"outputs":[]},{"cell_type":"code","source":["df.columns\n","angle(df['Thor'],df['Thor'])\n","# angle(df['Casablanca'],df['Casablanca'])\n","# angle(df['Insidious'],df['Insidious'])\n","# np.linalg()"],"metadata":{"colab":{"base_uri":"https://localhost:8080/"},"id":"c_LoRgzIKSRj","executionInfo":{"status":"ok","timestamp":1718023368518,"user_tz":-330,"elapsed":9,"user":{"displayName":"Akhil Teja BM","userId":"02238052875404682489"}},"outputId":"bc16d59b-ad8a-4d45-98a3-08e38b6e9d1e"},"execution_count":28,"outputs":[{"output_type":"execute_result","data":{"text/plain":["0.0455290776372261"]},"metadata":{},"execution_count":28}]},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":["# Task 6 - Create a function that returns the name of the movie that is most similar to a given movie"],"metadata":{"id":"BCSmjaWuGxIL"}},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":["### **Logic**\n","\n","- The angle between two vectors can be used as a measure of similarity between the two vectors. The smaller the angle between two vectors, the higher their similarity score, and vice versa.\n","- The larger the angle between two vectors, the lower their similarity score, and vice versa\n","\n","**What's Next ?**- We will create a function that returns the name of the movie which is most similar to a given movie"],"metadata":{"id":"kQ5RKlcWG2ws"}},{"cell_type":"code","source":["movies_rec = pd.Series(index=df.columns)\n","movies_rec"],"metadata":{"colab":{"base_uri":"https://localhost:8080/"},"id":"ivM25Yj4LXYR","executionInfo":{"status":"ok","timestamp":1718023368518,"user_tz":-330,"elapsed":8,"user":{"displayName":"Akhil Teja BM","userId":"02238052875404682489"}},"outputId":"cb657cc8-677b-41f6-8952-0eda014cb7da"},"execution_count":29,"outputs":[{"output_type":"execute_result","data":{"text/plain":["Thor                   NaN\n","Justice League         NaN\n","Spiderman              NaN\n","The Notebook           NaN\n","Casablanca             NaN\n","Beauty and the Beast   NaN\n","Dolittle               NaN\n","The Kissing Booth      NaN\n","Annabelle              NaN\n","Insidious              NaN\n","dtype: float64"]},"metadata":{},"execution_count":29}]},{"cell_type":"code","source":["user_movie = input('Enter a movie: ')\n","for movie in movies_rec.index :\n","    movies_rec.loc[movie] = angle(df[user_movie],df[movie])\n","movies_rec.sort_values().index[1]"],"metadata":{"colab":{"base_uri":"https://localhost:8080/","height":52},"id":"HP9Ap32fLz05","executionInfo":{"status":"ok","timestamp":1718023375872,"user_tz":-330,"elapsed":7358,"user":{"displayName":"Akhil Teja BM","userId":"02238052875404682489"}},"outputId":"c6fd54b8-67f0-4690-9f4b-069d3aaff32d"},"execution_count":30,"outputs":[{"name":"stdout","output_type":"stream","text":["Enter a movie: Thor\n"]},{"output_type":"execute_result","data":{"text/plain":["'Justice League'"],"application/vnd.google.colaboratory.intrinsic+json":{"type":"string"}},"metadata":{},"execution_count":30}]},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":["# Observations\n","\n","- The angle between the user ratings vectors for two particular movies is a measure of similarity between the movies\n","- The similarity score is not symmetric\n","- Rather it pertains to a specific case (such as a specific movie)\n","- This is because it is possible for X to be most similar to Y and Y to be most similar to Z\n","- This happens when X and Z are not as similar to each other as X and Y or Y and Z\n","- Transitivity, on the other hand, is a useful assumption for recommendations"],"metadata":{"id":"Ui6xODbHHLXN"}},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":["# Conclusions\n","\n","- From the user movie ratings data, we can extract movie ratings vectors and user ratings vectors. We can use magnitudes of movie ratings vectors for particular users to measure their interest in movies.\n","- We can use magnitudes of user ratings vectors for particular movies to measure their popularity.\n","- We can use the angle between movies ratings vectors of two users to measure their similarity.\n","- We can use the angle between user ratings vectors of two movies to measure their similarity.\n","- Using these measures, we can recommend similar movies to similar users\n","\n"," **This is a very basic look into the working methodology of recommender systems.**"],"metadata":{"id":"PDD9QveWHQpm"}},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":["Which is a better measurement of similarity in dot product , is it magnitude or angle?\n","\n","-\n","\n","Both the magnitude and angle of the dot product can provide information about the similarity between vectors, but they capture different aspects of similarity.\n","\n","Magnitude: The magnitude or length of a vector, which can be computed using the dot product, provides information about the overall scale or magnitude of the vectors. If the dot product of two vectors is large, it indicates that the vectors have similar magnitudes or are scaled proportionally. However, the magnitude alone does not capture the direction or orientation of the vectors.\n","\n","Angle: The angle between two vectors, which can be determined using the dot product, provides information about the alignment or orientation of the vectors. If the dot product is high, it suggests that the vectors are closely aligned or have a small angle between them. Conversely, if the dot product is low or zero, it indicates that the vectors are orthogonal or have a large angle between them.\n","\n","In terms of measuring similarity, it depends on the context and the specific problem you are trying to solve. Here are some considerations:\n","\n","Magnitude-based similarity: If the magnitude or scale of the vectors is crucial for your application, such as comparing the intensities of signals or the sizes of objects, then the magnitude of the dot product can be a suitable measure of similarity. For example, in image processing, comparing the magnitudes of color histograms can provide insights into the similarity of image content.\n","\n","Angle-based similarity: If the orientation or alignment of the vectors is more important for your application, such as comparing directions or orientations of objects, then the angle between the vectors (computed using the dot product) can be a more appropriate measure of similarity. For instance, in natural language processing, comparing the angles between word vectors in a semantic space can help determine the similarity of word meanings.\n","\n","In many cases, a combination of both magnitude and angle may be necessary to capture the full similarity between vectors. It's important to consider the specific context and requirements of your problem to determine which measure of similarity (magnitude, angle, or both) is more appropriate."],"metadata":{"id":"7SwchNPQQcrc"}}]}